course: biology
lesson: 404
title: The real animals of the Just So Stories: the whale, camel, rhino, leopard, elephant, kangaroo, armadillo and crab
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Rudyard Kipling's «Just So Stories» (1902) tell how animals «got» their shapes: the Whale his small throat, the Camel his hump, the Rhinoceros his folds, the Leopard his spots, the Elephant his trunk and the Kangaroo his long legs. They are funny made-up tales, and every reason they give is wrong.
explain: In real life no animal got its shape in one afternoon. Living things change very slowly over a great many generations: the young that are born a little better suited to their life survive more often and pass that on to their own young.
explain: And a body part that is stretched or hurt during a life is never passed on. A kangaroo chased all day would not have longer-legged babies, just as a footballer's baby is not born with strong legs.
explain: Scientists even borrowed Kipling's title: they call a neat explanation that nobody has tested «a just-so story». So when you read the book, ask two questions: what is true about this animal, and what is only the joke?
fact: Blue whales and humpbacks have no teeth: rows of baleen plates hang from the top jaw and sieve tiny krill and small fish out of huge mouthfuls of water, a bit like the sailor's grating.
fact: A baleen whale's throat is narrow for such a giant, and it could not swallow a person; the toothed sperm whale is different and swallows big squid whole.
fact: A camel's hump is a store of fat, not water; a thirsty camel can drink about a hundred litres in around ten minutes.
fact: Rhino skin is very thick, up to about five centimetres in places; the Indian rhino's deep folds make it look as if it wears plates of armour, and they let it bend and move.
fact: Rhinos wallow in mud, which cools them and protects their skin from sunburn and biting flies; no rhino ever took its skin off, and cake crumbs do not cause folds.
fact: A leopard is born with its rosettes (rings of dark spots) because the pattern is passed down from its parents, the way your eye colour is; every leopard's pattern is different, and the spotty coat hides it in the dappled shade of trees and long grass.
fact: An elephant's trunk is its nose and upper lip joined together, with no bones and tens of thousands of small muscle bundles; elephant ancestors tens of millions of years ago had only a short nose, and trunks grew longer slowly over millions of years.
fact: A red kangaroo can cover about eight metres in one bound; springy tendons in its back legs store energy like a pogo stick, so hopping fast costs it little effort.
fact: Kangaroos were hopping for millions of years before dingoes reached Australia, probably with seafaring people at least 3,500 years ago, so the Dingo cannot have given the Kangaroo its legs.
fact: Armadillos are mammals with hair on their bellies, not a mix of hedgehog and tortoise; «armadillo» is Spanish for «little armoured one», and only the three-banded armadillos can roll into a full ball.
fact: A crab's skeleton is on the outside; to grow it climbs out of its old shell, which is called moulting, and it hides for a few days while the new shell hardens.
fact: The rock hyrax from the Leopard's story looks like a big guinea pig, yet its closest living relatives are elephants and manatees; the quagga, a zebra striped only at the front, died out in 1883.
fact: Wild cats moved into the first farming villages about 10,000 years ago to hunt the mice in the grain stores, and they stayed more independent than dogs: that is the true heart of «the Cat that walked by himself».
fact: Dogs were tamed first, from wolves, at least 15,000 years ago; cows came about 10,000 years ago and horses only about 5,500 years ago, so Kipling's order Dog, Horse, Cow is not the real one.
fact: A cabbage white butterfly weighs much less than a gram, so its stamp could never shake a palace; butterflies taste with their feet.
exercise: What do blue whales use instead of teeth? | Baleen plates that sieve krill out of the water | Rows of small sharp teeth hidden behind the lips | A long sticky tongue that catches fish one at a time | Hard bony gums that crush shellfish
exercise: Could a blue whale swallow a sailor like the Whale in the story? | No, its throat is far too narrow | Yes, but only a very small sailor | Yes, it often swallows divers by mistake | No, because it has no throat at all
exercise: Which whale swallows big squid whole? | The toothed sperm whale | The baleen blue whale | The humpback whale | The grey whale that feeds on mud
exercise: What is inside a camel's hump? | Fat | Water | Air | Milk
exercise: Why do rhinos roll in mud? | To cool down and keep off sun and flies | To clean the cake crumbs out of their skin | To make their horns grow bigger and sharper | To hide from lions by looking like a rock
exercise: Which rhino has the deepest folds, like armour? | The Indian rhino | The white rhino | The black rhino | The woolly rhino of today
exercise: Where does a leopard's pattern come from? | Passed down from its parents | Fingerprints of a hunter | Standing in the shade of trees | The mud it rolls in as a cub
exercise: What is an elephant's trunk made of? | Nose and upper lip, with no bones | A long tongue covered in thick skin | Its nose with a long chain of small bones inside | Its lower lip, stretched out over the years
exercise: About how far can a red kangaroo go in one bound? | About eight metres | About one metre | About fifty metres | About a hundred metres
exercise: What helps a kangaroo hop cheaply, like a pogo stick? | Springy tendons in its back legs | Air sacs in its tail that push it up | Its pouch, which bounces it along | Extra bones in its front paws
exercise: What does «armadillo» mean in Spanish? | Little armoured one | Hedgehog and tortoise | Animal that rolls | Digger of the river
exercise: What kind of animal is an armadillo? | A mammal with bony plates | A reptile like a tortoise | A giant beetle with a hard shell | A kind of hedgehog from America
exercise: What is a crab doing when it moults? | Climbing out of its old shell to grow | Losing its legs in a fight | Laying its eggs on the beach | Changing its colour to hide
exercise: Which animals are the rock hyrax's closest living relatives? | Elephants and manatees | Rabbits and hares | Rats and guinea pigs | Monkeys and baboons
exercise: Why did wild cats first come to people's villages? | To hunt the mice in grain stores | Because people caught them with nets | To be fed milk by the women | To guard the sheep from wolves
exercise: Which animal did people tame first? | The dog | The horse | The cow | The cat
exercise: Which part of a butterfly can taste? | Its feet | Its wings | Its antennae only | Its eyes
test: «so the Camel got his hump for being lazy, that's what the book says» What is true? | The hump is a fat store that evolved slowly and helps camels live in deserts | Lazy camels really do grow bigger humps than hard-working ones | The hump is a water tank that fills up when a camel is idle | Camels got humps when people started riding them
test: A cartoon shows a blue whale gulping down a sailor whole. What is wrong with it? | Its throat is too narrow; it sieves krill | Nothing, blue whales often eat sailors | Blue whales have teeth and would bite him first | Blue whales live only in rivers, not at sea
test: Gran says the Rhino got his folds from cake crumbs. Which is the real reason? | Thick, folded skin grew over many generations and lets it bend | A heat wave made the rhino's skin shrink and wrinkle | The mud it lies in dried and cracked into folds | Rhinos got folds from scratching against palm-trees
test: «leopards just stand in shade till they get spotty, innit» What would you say? | No, a leopard cub is born with its spots | Yes, but only young leopards can do it | Yes, it takes about a month in deep shade | No, leopards get spots from a rash
test: What would happen if a crab never moulted? | It could not grow any bigger | It would grow too fast | Its shell would go soft for ever | It would turn into a lobster
test: «the crocodile pulled the elephant's nose and that's why it's long, my lil bro reckons» What is right? | Trunks grew slowly over millions of years; a stretched nose is not passed to babies | It is true, but it happened to every elephant, not one | Trunks grew because elephants stretched them to reach water every day | Elephants are born with short noses that a crocodile must pull
test: ★★ Dingoes came to Australia some thousands of years ago, but kangaroos were hopping millions of years before. What does this prove? | The Dingo cannot have given the Kangaroo its long legs | Dingoes must be older than kangaroos | Kangaroos were taught to hop by people | Kangaroos once had short legs a few thousand years ago
test: «armadillo is half hedgehog half tortoise, the book says so» What is true? | It is a mammal of its own kind, with hair on its belly | It really is a mix of the two animals | It is a reptile, like the tortoise | It is a kind of hedgehog that lives in America
test: A friend wants to train a hedgehog to swim until it turns into an armadillo. What is wrong? | Practising cannot change one animal into another kind | Hedgehogs can do it, but it takes about a year | It only works if a tortoise helps | Armadillos are just hedgehogs that learned to swim
test: Kipling's Cat «walks by himself». Which real fact matches the story best? | Cats tamed themselves by hunting mice and stayed independent | Cats were the very first animals that people tamed | Cats will only live with people who give them milk | Cats were caught wild and trained like horses
test: «bro the butterfly stamped and the whole palace vanished, physics is cooked» Why can't it happen? | It weighs much less than a gram, so its stamp is tiny | Butterflies have no feet, so they cannot stamp at all | Palaces are built on rock, which butterflies cannot shake | Butterflies stamp only in cold weather, when it is weak
test: ★★★ Kipling's order of taming is Dog, Horse, Cow. If you list the real order from the oldest to the newest, which animal moves? | The horse, which comes after the cow | The dog, which comes after the horse | The cow, which comes first of all | None, Kipling's order is right
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the feeding apparatus of the blue whale? | It possesses baleen plates, by which minute krill are strained from the water | It possesses large teeth, by which seals and penguins are seized | It possesses a gullet capable of admitting a seafarer entire | It possesses a sticky tongue, by which fish are captured singly
exam: Identify the principal reason why the hypothesis that the Elephant obtained its trunk through stretching by a crocodile is erroneous. | Bodily changes acquired during a lifetime are not inherited by offspring | Crocodiles are incapable of grasping the nose of any animal larger than a goat | The Elephant possessed a trunk before crocodiles came into existence | The nasal tissue of elephants contains bone and cannot be stretched
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the armadillo. | It is a mammal whose name signifies «little armoured one» in Spanish | It is a reptile descended from the union of a hedgehog and a tortoise | It is an insect of exceptional size inhabiting the Amazon | It is a bird which has lost the power of flight
exam: Ascertain which animal was first domesticated by human beings. | The dog, descended from the wolf | The horse, upon the steppes | The cow, descended from the aurochs | The cat, in the grain stores of the first farmers
